approval record. Approval request IDs resolve uniquely to typed review requests;
subjects and required reviewer agree with that request. Linked feedback covers
those subjects and the same request/reviewer. An approved approval record needs
authenticated, matched feedback with an approved or satisfied conditional outcome.
Matched feedback is independently checked against its retained claimed request,
run, subject, draft, source versions and responder role; flags alone are insufficient.
Where a detached artifact binding exists, claimed artifact paths/hashes must match
it exactly and the retained bytes. Without such a binding, artifact claims cannot
be marked matched. An empty artifact claim in Stage 06 covers the named draft
subjects only; it does not demonstrate approval of final artifact bytes.
These are consistency checks; U13 must establish trusted identity/channel,
authority, applicability and actual condition satisfaction. Conditional
feedback requires `conditions_satisfied: true` and nonempty
`condition_evidence_ids` on the approval record, established by U13 against the
authenticated conditions; it does not become approved merely by having a
conditional outcome.

Feedback preserves `claimed_*` request/run/subject/draft/source/artifact bindings
separately from resolved `subject_ids`/`request_id`. Unknown/old/mismatched claims
remain retained with `match_status: unresolved`, empty resolved links and no
approval transition. This avoids forcing old claimed IDs into the current graph.
Old claims cannot be rewritten or flagged matched to transfer approval. U13 owns
facts/source/scope/conditions revalidation and any separately evidenced current
decision or response; the retained old response stays bound to its original draft.
Requests preserve every R6 field, including source versions, subject systems,
question, reviewer, draft/run and delivery status. `sent` requires delivery
evidence; tool permission to send is never established by a record.

